/** @page Keyboard Keyboard Shortcuts

%DarkMark keyboard shortcuts:

Key							| Saves Modified Marks First	| Description
----------------------------|-------------------------------|------------
@p a (or @p LEFT)			| yes							| Go to previous image.  If at the first image, do nothing.
@p d (or @p RIGHT)			| yes							| Go to the next image.  If at the last image, do nothing.
@p w						|								| Move/Navigate up (or increase threshold).
@p s						|								| Move/Navigate down (or decrease threshold).
@p PAGEUP					| yes							| Go to the previous image which has not yet been marked.  (Also see @p 'Y'.)
@p PAGEDOWN					| yes							| Go to the next image which has not yet been marked.  (Also see @p 'y'.)
@p HOME						| yes							| Go to the first image.
@p END						| yes							| Go to the last image.
@p ENTER (or @p RETURN)		|								| In merge mode, confirms the current frame's selection.
@p x (or @p DEL)			|								| Delete the currently selected marking.
@p v (or @p SHIFT + @p DEL)	| no							| Delete the current image from disk.  Moves the image to the trash, removing it from the data set.  Be careful, this @em does @em not prompt!
@p ESC						| yes							| Cancels "zoom", cancels merge mode, otherwise quit from DarkMark.
@p F1						|								| About DarkMark.
@p TAB						|								| Move focus to the next marking.
@p SHIFT + @p TAB			|								| Move focus to the previous marking.
@p +						|								| Zoom image +10%.  Can be pressed repeatedly.
@p SHIFT + @p +				|								| Zoom image 500%.
@p -						|								| Zoom image -10%.  Can be pressed repeatedly.
@p CTRL + @p c (or @p CTRL + @p INSERT)		|				| Copy the current selected annotation (or all annotations if none are selected) to the clipboard.
@p CTRL + @p v (or @p SHIFT + @p INSERT)	|				| Paste the annotations from the clipboard into the current image.
@p SPACE					|								| Toggle between automatic zoom and manual zoom (see @p '-', @p '+', @p 'ESC' keys, and @p CTRL below).
@p ` (backtick)				| yes							| Toggle merge mode. Select one or more bounding boxes in the first frame, then corresponding boxes in the second frame, and interpolate all pairs across intermediate frames.
@p q (lowercase)			|								| Accept all Darknet predictions (if any) for the current image as actual markings.
@p Q (uppercase) / @p SHIFT + @p q	|						| Accept only the currently-selected prediction and discard all other predictions.
@p b (lowercase)			|								| Toggle @em bold mode.  All markings will be displayed in "bold", making it easier to review.
@p B (uppercase) / @p SHIFT + @p b	|						| Toggle @em shade mode.  All marked rectangles will be shaded in a semi-transparent colour.
@p c (lowercase)			|								| Display the 'class' menu.
@p C (uppercase) / @p SHIFT + @p c	|						| Clear all marks for the current image.
@p SHIFT + @p d				|								| Snap the current annotation, or all annotations on the current image.
@p CTRL + @p d				|								| Toggle auto-snapping.
@p e						|								| Miscellaneous settings window.
@p f						| yes							| Filter window.
@p h						| yes							| Cycle between the various heatmaps for each class.
@p H						| yes							| Toggle loading and displaying the heatmap from Darknet.
@p j						| yes							| Jump to a different image.
@p l						|								| Cycle between the various label modes (on, off, auto).
@p m						|								| Toggle hiding/showing the marks.
@p n						| yes							| Mark image as empty ("none") and move to the next image.
@p o 						| yes							| Mass delete predictions from multiple images.
@p p						|								| Toggle Darknet predictions.
@p r (lowercase)			| yes							| Randomly shuffle the images and load the first image.
@p R (uppercase)			| yes							| Sort images alphabetically.
@p CTRL + @p s				|								| Save the current image with markings to a new filename (screenshot).
@p SHIFT + @p s				|								| Save screenshot at full 100% size.
@p t						| yes							| Toggle image tiling.
@p SHIFT + @p w				|								| Toggle black-and-white mode.
@p y (lowercase)			|								| Copy marks from previous (alphabetical) marked up image.
@p Y (uppercase)			|								| Copy marks from next (alphabetical) marked up image.
@p z						| yes							| Zoom into the image to review annotations.
@p 1-9, 0					|								| Set class ID: key 1 = class 0, key 2 = class 1, ..., key 0 = class 9.
@p CTRL + @p 1-9, 0			|								| Set class ID: key ctrl+1 = class 10, ..., key ctrl+0 = class 19.
@p ALT + @p 1-9, 0			|								| Set class ID: key alt+1 = class 20, ..., key alt+0 = class 29.

In addition to these keyboard shortcuts, note the following mouse behaviour:

@li When you click on an existing mark, the application remembers both the class and the size of the selected mark.  If you then double-click on an image, DarkMark assumes you want to create a new mark of the same size, and with the same class.  The new mark is positioned so the mouse click is at the center.  This can be useful if images have a large number of repeating objects.

@li In merge mode (activated with @p `), you can select one or more bounding boxes for interpolation:
  - Click on bounding boxes to select them (they will be highlighted in purple)
  - Press @p ENTER to confirm the first frame's selection, or simply navigate to another image
  - Navigate to the second frame and select corresponding bounding boxes in the same order
  - Press @p ENTER again to start the interpolation process
  - All selected bounding box pairs will be interpolated across the intermediate frames

@li While creating a new mark or resizing an existing mark, you can hold down @p SHIFT to alter the snap behaviour.  If snap is disabled, then holding down @p SHIFT when you let go of the mouse button will temporarily enable snapping.  If snap is enabled, then holding down @p SHIFT when you let go of the mouse button will temporarily disable snapping.  (Snapping is usually toggled with @p d and @p D.)

@li While dragging the mouse on a zoomed-in image, hold down @p CTRL to pan the image.  (Or press @p SPACE to zoom out and back in.)

@li Hold down @p CTRL and scroll the mouse wheel to zoom in/out of the image.  The zoom will be centered on the mouse cursor position.

*/
